## What the results mean

These are promising, very one-sided results at THIS control. They are insufficient
for an absolute rating or a human/FIDE claim. The reference CCRL 40/15 ratings
identify opponents; our time control, host, runtime, build and hash settings differ.
Do not add a score-derived Elo difference to a published CCRL rating and call that
Mitchell's established rating.

The old unrated Level 35/50 matches and pre-hash-fix speed figures are historical.
They must not be pooled with this repaired-engine run. The earlier faulty campaign
is separately archived under validation/regressions/failed-campaign/.

## Fastest defensible route to a rating estimate

1. Choose the scale first: a local computer-engine rating under stated conditions,
   a CCRL-comparable estimate, and a human FIDE rating are different claims.
2. Run short colour-paired pilots against stronger, independent opponents. Seek
   opponents giving about 35-65% score at the intended final control. The earlier Fruit/MinimalChess
   opponents were too one-sided to narrow an upper rating efficiently. Choose
   final opponents before collecting the final rating sample; publish pilot games
   separately instead of selectively mixing favourable pilot results into it.
3. Freeze the engine, versions, openings, control, hash/thread settings, referee,
   runtime and host details. Use the SAME control for the final pool and reference
   matches. For a CCRL-comparable claim, follow the relevant list's published
   control/hardware methodology; one second per move is not CCRL 40/15.
4. Concentrate roughly 200-400 final games against 2-4 competitive opponents,
   with balanced colours, varied openings, and no pondering. Run games in parallel
   only within available CPU and memory limits; never oversubscribe to gain speed.
5. Fit the pool with an established estimator such as Ordo or BayesElo. Publish
   raw PGNs, anchored references and uncertainty. Include opponent calibration
   uncertainty and differences in test conditions, not just game-sampling error.

Around a 50% score, an idealized independent decisive-game model gives about
+/-48 Elo sampling error at 95% for 200 games, or +/-34 for 400. These are planning
figures, NOT this engine's Elo, NOT a guarantee, and NOT an overall confidence
interval: draws, opening pairs, anchors and systematic differences change it.
SPRT can efficiently decide whether a version improved; it does not by itself
establish an absolute Elo rating. A computer pool alone cannot establish FIDE Elo.

