## Repeat the recorded 200-game protocol on another machine

External programs are test-only; no third-party engine, referee or compatibility
library is distributed with the MIT game. Install independently:

- Fastchess 1.8.2-alpha (the recorded build reports f618e34, July 26, 2026).
- Fruit 2.1, built from the source revision in campaign.json using its supplied
  Makefile. Our x86-64 build can differ from the historical CCRL executable.
- MinimalChess 0.6, the official Linux release. Its older .NET runtime may require
  a separately installed compatibility libssl1.1 and invariant-globalization
  setting on modern Linux; resolve runtime setup before collecting games.
- Node.js v24.22.1 for the closest match to the recorded runtime, and Python.

Opponent sources and reference citations are recorded in:

    validation/independent/results-58/campaign.json
    validation/independent-campaign.md

Prepare a NEW, empty output directory with local executable paths:

    python scripts/prepare-benchmark.py \
      --out /tmp/mitchell-repeat \
      --fastchess /absolute/path/to/fastchess \
      --fruit /absolute/path/to/fruit \
      --minimal /absolute/path/to/MinimalChessEngine \
      --node /absolute/path/to/node \
      --revision bdb5873406a2b3336523f3a19f16c4a1bc9ebd46

This extracts the tested HTML, UCI adapter, loader and opening file from Git,
freezes them in the new directory, hashes the supplied binaries and records local
runtime versions. It refuses to overwrite an existing run. Environment variables
are inherited from your terminal but are not copied into the manifest; record any
explicit compatibility-library setup you use when publishing your own results.
The runner verifies the frozen engine, adapter, openings and opponent hashes.
Time-limited play need not produce identical moves or outcomes on another machine;
this reproduces the protocol and inputs, not a predetermined score.

Then run the command printed by the preparer, normally:

    python scripts/independent-campaign.py /tmp/mitchell-repeat/campaign.json

Protocol: 100 games per opponent, paired colours across the same 50 openings,
1 second per move, equal 250 ms scheduling grace, no books or pondering, no
score-based resignation/draw adjudication, and no move-limit draws. Fruit and
MinimalChess use 32 MB hash; Mitchell retains approximately 36 MB. Two games
run concurrently, in twenty-game batches. Actual move times remain in PGN;
equal time limits do not imply equal time actually used. A full run takes hours.

The runner saves PGNs, logs, configuration, per-game checkpoints, status.json and
summary.json. It pauses on abnormal termination. For an ordinary interruption,
with unchanged frozen inputs and no unresolved fault:

    python scripts/independent-campaign.py /tmp/mitchell-repeat/campaign.json --resume

An unresolved recorded forfeit will trigger the safety pause again. Do not edit
or delete games merely to bypass it. Review the cause and publish a new protocol
if changing an adapter, opponent, adjudication or clock policy. In particular,
normalizing MinimalChess's uppercase promotion output would be a separately
labelled adapter experiment, not an unmodified repeat of the recorded one.

